**assistant**:
> `xtable`’s `digits` argument is a bit quirky. In your call
> 
> ```r
> print(xtable(tbl, digits=2), include.rownames=F)
> ```
> 
> `digits=2` is being interpreted, but not in the way you expect.
> 
> ### How `digits` works in `xtable`
> 
> From `?xtable`:
> 
> - `digits` can be:
>   - a single number, or
>   - a vector of length `ncol(x) + 1`
> 
> And the **first** element corresponds to the **row names** column; the rest correspond to each column in `tbl`.
> 
> So:
> 
> - If you pass a **single** number `d`, `xtable` expands it to a vector of length `ncol(tbl) + 1`, all equal to `d`.
> - That means all numeric *and* rowname columns get the same number of digits.
> 
> In your LaTeX output:
> 
> ```latex
> persist & 0\% & 5\% & 50\% & 95\% & 100\% & miniso & frac.miniso & maxiso & frac.maxiso \\ 
> ...
> 0 & -14.565260342583 & -2.43522013955961 & ...
> ```
> 
> The numbers are not rounded to 2 digits, which suggests one of these is happening:
> 
> 1. **Non-numeric columns are present** and `xtable` leaves them as-is (`miniso`, `maxiso` look like character).
> 2. `digits` is being overridden at `print()` time.
> 3. `tbl` is not a plain numeric matrix/data frame in the way you think.
> 
> ### Things to try
> 
> 1. **Specify per-column digits explicitly**
> 
>    Count your columns, including the (optional) rownames column. You have 10 visible columns; if `include.rownames=FALSE`, `xtable` still thinks in terms of `ncol(tbl)` and a potential rowname column at index 1 of `digits`.
> 
>    For your table:  
>    - Column 1: `persist` (probably numeric)  
>    - Columns 2–6: quantiles (numeric)  
>    - Column 7: `miniso` (character)  
>    - Column 8: `frac.miniso` (numeric)  
>    - Column 9: `maxiso` (character)  
>    - Column 10: `frac.maxiso` (numeric)
> 
>    You can do:
> 
>    ```r
>    d <- c(
>      0,  # rownames (even if you don't print them)
>      2,  # persist
>      2, 2, 2, 2, 2,  # 0%, 5%, 50%, 95%, 100%
>      0,  # miniso (character/factor; digits ignored)
>      2,  # frac.miniso
>      0,  # maxiso (character/factor; digits ignored)
>      2   # frac.maxiso
>    )
> 
>    print(xtable(tbl, digits = d), include.rownames = FALSE)
>    ```
> 
> 2. **Check the structure of `tbl`**
> 
>    ```r
>    str(tbl)
>    ```
> 
>    If some columns are `character` or `factor`, `digits` won’t apply to them; `xtable` just prints the raw string.
> 
> 3. **Avoid overwriting at print time**
> 
>    Make sure you’re not doing:
> 
>    ```r
>    print(xtable(tbl), digits = 2)
>    ```
> 
>    because `print.xtable` also has a `digits` argument, which can override the one stored in the `xtable` object. In your pasted line you don’t, so that’s probably fine, but it’s worth checking your actual code.
> 
> 4. **Test on a simple numeric example**
> 
>    To confirm that your version of `xtable` is behaving as documented:
> 
>    ```r
>    library(xtable)
>    m <- matrix(runif(6), ncol = 3)
>    print(xtable(m, digits = 2), include.rownames = FALSE)
>    ```
> 
>    If that gives 2 decimal places, then the issue is with the types/structure of `tbl`, not `digits` itself.
> 
> ---
> 
> If you paste the output of `str(tbl)` I can give you an exact `digits` vector that will do what you want.
